Let $n\geq 1$, let $A\in\mathrm{GL}_n(\mathbb{R})$, and let $\Gamma\subset\mathbb{R}^n$ be a full-rank lattice. A measurable set $W\subset\mathbb{R}^n$ is called an $(A,\Gamma)$-wavelet set if both of the following hold:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(t)] the translates $\{W+\gamma\}_{\gamma\in\Gamma}$ form a partition of $\mathbb{R}^n$ modulo Lebesgue-null sets;
\item[(d)] the dilates $\{A^j W\}_{j\in\mathbb{Z}}$ form a partition of $\mathbb{R}^n$ modulo Lebesgue-null sets.
\end{enumerate}
Give a complete characterization of those pairs $(A,\Gamma)$ for which an $(A,\Gamma)$-wavelet set exists.

